![](/images/articles/save-money-1.jpeg)

Buy our software and you'll save money! It's obvious and compelling! It's quantifiable! It's clear!

So why does this value proposition fail so often? Our customers say they need to control costs, they're being asked to do more with less, their budgets are tight — why doesn't cost-cutting work as a primary value proposition?

Why doesn't cost-cutting work as a primary value proposition?

Here are eight factors weighing against using cost-cutting as your lead value proposition for enterprise software.

(1) You're competing with every other cost-cutting initiative. There are lots of ways companies can save money. When you lead with cost-cutting, you compete with every one of them.

(2) Spending to save is a last resort. Lots of ways to save do not require you to spend first. You can reduce headcount, cut travel, starve marketing... all of which are "free" ways to cut costs.

(3) Cost-cutting will be important next quarter too. Cost-cutting is an evergreen priority, but it usually lacks urgency, especially when you're asking for up-front investment for a savings in future quarters.

(4) Cost savings doesn't resonate with the rank-and-file. Most enterprise sales are consensus-driven, and start with (or require buy-in from) the rank-and-file. Just like your salespeople don't track your CAC ratio and your customer success reps don't obsess about gross margins, your customer's individual contributors aren't typically motivated by cost control.

(5) Innovation requires inspiration, and no one gets inspired by cost-cutting. Many software purchases are driven by a champion's desire to innovate within their company. You're looking to inspire them to achieve and improve. It's hard to tap into inspiration with a cost-cutting message.

(6) Bundling is also effective cost-cutting, and it's antithetical to innovation. Enterprise IT departments want to consolidate suppliers to get greater efficiency and buying power. They'd rather use a second-rate solution from an existing vendor than an innovative solution from a new supplier. In this environment, it's tough to pitch cost-cutting as consistent with innovation, unless you're Oracle, Amazon, Microsoft, or Salesforce pitching consolidation *as* innovation.

(7) No one signs up for the cost savings. Your software solution doesn't cut costs on its own; it requires process or structural change in the business to take advantage of those efficiencies. *Someone has to give up headcount or budget to realize the benefits of your solution.* The CFO will say: "Jim wants to buy this software because it will save headcount. So when Josie shows me the names of the 20 people she'll fire, I'll sign off on the software purchase." You'd better have amazing consensus and buy-in from your stakeholders to navigate this challenge.

(8) Risk is a factor in decision-making. As compelling as your case may be, you are asking customers to take a chance on a cost savings initiative. You may be asking a customer to take the risk on you as a supplier, on an innovative solution, on the cost of disruption and change, on the likelihood the technology will be successful, and on the ability to achieve the realized cost savings through process change. Your cost savings argument may be compelling, but does it hold up on a risk-adjusted basis?

These are the reasons that using cost savings as your *primary* value proposition is a difficult path. In[ part 2](https://www.linkedin.com/pulse/perils-save-money-message-part-2-steve-bamberger-m0cnc), we'll talk about when to lead with cost savings anyway, and how to leverage cost savings as a supplementary value proposition.
